1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,560
Drop a blue copper
sulfate crystal into water

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:03,640
and watch.

3
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:07,200
Blue streaks drift off it,
the crystal shrinks,

4
00:00:07,320 --> 00:00:10,080
and soon the whole beaker is evenly pale

5
00:00:10,240 --> 00:00:10,640
blue.

6
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:14,080
That blue colour belongs
to the copper ions,

7
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:16,200
so you're watching ions
spread out through the

8
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:16,720
water.

9
00:00:17,280 --> 00:00:19,680
But in a crystal, ions are held tightly

10
00:00:19,800 --> 00:00:20,320
together.

11
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:22,000
So what pulls them out?

12
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:24,240
And why do some salts make the water

13
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:26,560
hot, while others make it cold?

14
00:00:27,767 --> 00:00:30,246
An ionic solid like table salt is a

15
00:00:30,287 --> 00:00:30,886
lattice:

16
00:00:31,447 --> 00:00:34,166
a regular stack
of positive sodium ions and

17
00:00:34,327 --> 00:00:37,446
negative chloride ions,
where each ion is held

18
00:00:37,527 --> 00:00:39,526
by the pull of all the opposite charges

19
00:00:39,607 --> 00:00:40,166
around it.

20
00:00:40,887 --> 00:00:44,006
Water molecules are polar:
the oxygen end is

21
00:00:44,087 --> 00:00:46,726
slightly negative,
and the two hydrogen ends are

22
00:00:46,767 --> 00:00:47,766
slightly positive.

23
00:00:48,247 --> 00:00:50,086
You met both of these in Year 11.

24
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:54,100
Water molecules crowd
around the crystal and turn

25
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:57,140
to face the ions: their oxygen ends point

26
00:00:57,180 --> 00:01:00,020
at the positive sodium
ions, and their hydrogen

27
00:01:00,100 --> 00:01:03,140
ends point at the negative chloride ions.

28
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:05,700
This pull between an ion
and the oppositely

29
00:01:05,780 --> 00:01:08,100
charged end of a water molecule is called

30
00:01:08,180 --> 00:01:10,740
an ion-dipole attraction.

31
00:01:10,820 --> 00:01:12,660
An ion on a corner of the crystal

32
00:01:12,740 --> 00:01:15,300
has the fewest neighbours
holding it, so it's

33
00:01:15,380 --> 00:01:17,380
the easiest to pull away.

34
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:21,300
Once it's out, water molecules
surround it completely.

35
00:01:21,380 --> 00:01:23,460
An ion wrapped in water like this is

36
00:01:23,540 --> 00:01:26,660
hydrated, and that's what aqueous means.

37
00:01:27,867 --> 00:01:31,386
So dissolving salt is written
as: sodium chloride

38
00:01:31,467 --> 00:01:35,466
solid gives sodium ions
and chloride ions, both

39
00:01:35,547 --> 00:01:36,186
aqueous.

40
00:01:36,827 --> 00:01:39,066
Notice that no new ions are made.

41
00:01:39,467 --> 00:01:41,786
The ions were already
there in the crystal.

42
00:01:42,187 --> 00:01:45,386
Dissolving just separates them,
which is the dissociation

43
00:01:45,467 --> 00:01:47,946
you met last video, and wraps each one

44
00:01:48,027 --> 00:01:48,666
in water.

45
00:01:49,147 --> 00:01:51,706
So there are no salt
molecules floating around.

46
00:01:52,934 --> 00:01:55,973
Dissolving also involves
energy, and you can count

47
00:01:56,054 --> 00:01:57,533
it in two steps.

48
00:01:57,814 --> 00:02:00,533
Step one: pulling the ions away from each

49
00:02:00,654 --> 00:02:03,973
other costs energy, because
opposite charges attract.

50
00:02:04,454 --> 00:02:05,973
Energy goes in.

51
00:02:06,294 --> 00:02:09,173
Step two: when water
molecules cling to the

52
00:02:09,254 --> 00:02:11,813
separated ions, energy is released.

53
00:02:12,214 --> 00:02:13,493
Energy comes out.

54
00:02:14,054 --> 00:02:16,853
In a real crystal both
happen together, but

55
00:02:16,934 --> 00:02:19,093
splitting them lets us count the energy.

56
00:02:19,654 --> 00:02:22,373
The overall heat change
when one mole dissolves

57
00:02:22,534 --> 00:02:25,013
is the enthalpy of solution, and it's just

58
00:02:25,094 --> 00:02:26,933
the two steps added together.

59
00:02:27,254 --> 00:02:29,813
If more energy comes out than goes in,

60
00:02:29,894 --> 00:02:31,973
the extra heats the water.

61
00:02:32,054 --> 00:02:34,453
If more goes in than comes out, that

62
00:02:34,654 --> 00:02:36,533
energy is taken from the water, and it

63
00:02:36,614 --> 00:02:37,493
gets colder.

64
00:02:38,700 --> 00:02:40,220
Here are real numbers.

65
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:43,500
Calcium chloride has
an enthalpy of solution of

66
00:02:43,660 --> 00:02:45,980
minus 81 kilojoules per mole.

67
00:02:46,540 --> 00:02:50,460
Minus means energy comes
out: hydrating its small,

68
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:53,900
doubly charged calcium ions
releases more energy than

69
00:02:53,940 --> 00:02:55,020
the lattice costs.

70
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:57,900
That's why some instant
hot packs use calcium

71
00:02:57,980 --> 00:02:58,780
chloride.

72
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:02,700
Ammonium nitrate is plus 25.7.

73
00:03:02,780 --> 00:03:05,740
The lattice costs more
than hydration pays back,

74
00:03:05,820 --> 00:03:07,300
so the water gets colder.

75
00:03:07,580 --> 00:03:10,220
That's what's inside
many instant cold packs.

76
00:03:11,434 --> 00:03:12,753
And table salt?

77
00:03:13,034 --> 00:03:14,793
Each of its two steps is close to

78
00:03:14,874 --> 00:03:18,473
800 kilojoules per mole,
but they almost cancel,

79
00:03:18,514 --> 00:03:21,113
leaving only plus 3.9.

80
00:03:21,194 --> 00:03:23,753
The difference between
two big numbers sets the

81
00:03:23,954 --> 00:03:26,553
temperature change, not
either one on its own.

82
00:03:27,767 --> 00:03:28,646
Here's a puzzle.

83
00:03:29,127 --> 00:03:32,566
If ammonium nitrate takes
in energy to dissolve,

84
00:03:32,647 --> 00:03:34,366
why does it dissolve at all?

85
00:03:34,727 --> 00:03:37,686
Remember from the enthalpy
and entropy video: a

86
00:03:37,767 --> 00:03:40,726
change is favoured by
releasing heat, and also

87
00:03:40,807 --> 00:03:41,846
by spreading out.

88
00:03:42,487 --> 00:03:44,726
Ions locked in a lattice
spread out through

89
00:03:44,807 --> 00:03:47,446
the water when they
dissolve, a big increase

90
00:03:47,527 --> 00:03:48,166
in entropy.

91
00:03:48,727 --> 00:03:51,846
For ammonium nitrate,
the spreading out wins over

92
00:03:51,887 --> 00:03:52,806
the energy cost.

93
00:03:53,287 --> 00:03:55,446
So you can't predict
whether a salt dissolves

94
00:03:55,527 --> 00:03:57,286
from its energy change alone.

95
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:00,980
Now keep adding salt to the same water,

96
00:04:01,060 --> 00:04:02,900
at the same temperature.

97
00:04:02,980 --> 00:04:06,500
At first every crystal
disappears: the solution is

98
00:04:06,660 --> 00:04:07,940
unsaturated.

99
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:11,060
Eventually some solid stays
on the bottom, however

100
00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:12,260
long you wait.

101
00:04:12,340 --> 00:04:15,220
Now it's saturated: it holds the most it

102
00:04:15,300 --> 00:04:16,980
can at that temperature.

103
00:04:17,060 --> 00:04:19,300
It looks as if dissolving has stopped.

104
00:04:19,380 --> 00:04:20,100
But has it?

105
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:21,940
Zoom in.

106
00:04:22,020 --> 00:04:24,820
Ions keep leaving the crystal at a steady

107
00:04:24,900 --> 00:04:25,300
rate.

108
00:04:25,780 --> 00:04:28,420
But dissolved ions also
bump into the crystal

109
00:04:28,500 --> 00:04:29,380
and stick to it.

110
00:04:29,860 --> 00:04:31,540
That's crystallising.

111
00:04:31,860 --> 00:04:34,340
At first there are few dissolved ions, so

112
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:35,860
crystallising is slow.

113
00:04:36,500 --> 00:04:38,980
As more ions dissolve,
they hit the crystal

114
00:04:39,060 --> 00:04:42,180
more often, so crystallising
speeds up, until the

115
00:04:42,340 --> 00:04:43,460
two rates are equal.

116
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:46,980
From then on ions still leave and rejoin,

117
00:04:47,020 --> 00:04:49,220
but the amount dissolved stays the same.

118
00:04:49,780 --> 00:04:52,340
That's a dynamic equilibrium,
so it gets the

119
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:53,380
two-way arrow.

120
00:04:53,780 --> 00:04:56,100
And its K, with the solid left out,

121
00:04:56,180 --> 00:04:58,500
is the Ksp from last video.

122
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:02,140
How do we know the swapping never stops?

123
00:05:02,420 --> 00:05:05,220
Add a crystal made with
radioactive ions, which

124
00:05:05,260 --> 00:05:08,020
a detector can track,
to a saturated solution

125
00:05:08,060 --> 00:05:10,980
of the same salt, potassium bromide.

126
00:05:11,060 --> 00:05:14,820
The amount dissolved doesn't
change, yet radioactivity soon

127
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:16,660
turns up in the solution.

128
00:05:16,820 --> 00:05:19,540
So ions must keep leaving the solid while

129
00:05:19,620 --> 00:05:21,060
others keep rejoining it.

130
00:05:22,267 --> 00:05:22,986
One trap.

131
00:05:23,547 --> 00:05:25,226
What if you tip more salt into a

132
00:05:25,307 --> 00:05:26,786
saturated solution?

133
00:05:27,067 --> 00:05:28,866
The solution doesn't change.

134
00:05:29,067 --> 00:05:31,786
The extra solid just
joins the pile, because

135
00:05:31,827 --> 00:05:33,946
the water already holds as much as it

136
00:05:34,107 --> 00:05:35,506
can at that temperature.

137
00:05:35,787 --> 00:05:38,186
In K terms, the solid is left out

138
00:05:38,227 --> 00:05:40,986
of the expression,
so adding more doesn't change

139
00:05:41,067 --> 00:05:41,866
Q.

140
00:05:41,947 --> 00:05:45,306
Q still equals K, so there's no shift.

141
00:05:45,627 --> 00:05:49,066
Temperature is different,
because it changes K itself.

142
00:05:49,147 --> 00:05:53,386
Potassium nitrate dissolves
endothermically, so warming its saturated

143
00:05:53,467 --> 00:05:56,426
solution raises K, and more dissolves.

144
00:05:57,634 --> 00:05:58,993
Two lines to remember.

145
00:05:59,554 --> 00:06:02,473
Lattice costs, hydration pays.

146
00:06:02,754 --> 00:06:05,713
Whichever is bigger decides
whether the water warms

147
00:06:05,794 --> 00:06:06,713
or cools.

148
00:06:06,914 --> 00:06:10,993
And saturated means
equal rates, not stopped.

149
00:06:12,200 --> 00:06:12,840
Your turn.

150
00:06:13,480 --> 00:06:16,760
When ammonium chloride dissolves
in water, the temperature

151
00:06:16,840 --> 00:06:20,320
falls from 22 degrees to 17 degrees.

152
00:06:20,600 --> 00:06:23,520
Explain this using the two energy steps.

153
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:25,160
Pause here to answer.

154
00:06:35,567 --> 00:06:39,086
The water cooled, so dissolving
ammonium chloride is

155
00:06:39,167 --> 00:06:40,286
endothermic.

156
00:06:40,607 --> 00:06:43,086
Separating its ions from
the lattice takes in

157
00:06:43,207 --> 00:06:46,206
more energy than hydrating
them releases, so the

158
00:06:46,367 --> 00:06:48,646
extra energy is taken from the water.

159
00:06:49,007 --> 00:06:51,966
If you wrote that
hydration takes in energy,

160
00:06:52,047 --> 00:06:55,886
flip it: water clinging
to ions always releases

161
00:06:56,127 --> 00:06:56,526
energy.

162
00:06:57,734 --> 00:06:58,373
Last one.

163
00:06:58,654 --> 00:07:01,413
You add more water to a saturated sodium

164
00:07:01,494 --> 00:07:04,173
chloride solution that
still has solid on the

165
00:07:04,214 --> 00:07:04,893
bottom.

166
00:07:05,174 --> 00:07:08,213
You stir and wait, at
the same temperature,

167
00:07:08,294 --> 00:07:10,213
and some solid is still left at the

168
00:07:10,294 --> 00:07:10,973
end.

169
00:07:11,334 --> 00:07:13,653
What happens to the mass of solid, and

170
00:07:13,734 --> 00:07:16,213
to the final concentration
of the solution?

171
00:07:16,534 --> 00:07:17,893
Pause here to answer.

172
00:07:28,300 --> 00:07:30,460
The mass of solid goes down.

173
00:07:30,700 --> 00:07:34,380
Adding water lowers the ion
concentrations, so Q

174
00:07:34,460 --> 00:07:37,420
falls below K, and more solid dissolves.

175
00:07:37,900 --> 00:07:40,620
It keeps dissolving until
the solution is saturated

176
00:07:40,700 --> 00:07:43,420
again, so the final
concentration is the same

177
00:07:43,500 --> 00:07:44,340
as before.

178
00:07:44,620 --> 00:07:46,860
If you said it ends up more dilute,

179
00:07:46,940 --> 00:07:50,140
check: solid is still
there, so it's saturated

180
00:07:50,220 --> 00:07:52,220
again, at the same temperature.

181
00:07:53,434 --> 00:07:56,873
So when an ionic solid
dissolves, water molecules

182
00:07:57,034 --> 00:07:59,393
pull the ions out and hydrate them.

183
00:07:59,674 --> 00:08:02,393
To explain a temperature
change, compare the cost

184
00:08:02,474 --> 00:08:04,953
of separating the ions
with the payback from

185
00:08:05,034 --> 00:08:05,833
hydrating them.

186
00:08:06,314 --> 00:08:07,993
And with solid on the bottom, it's an

187
00:08:08,074 --> 00:08:10,713
equilibrium, so use K and Q.

188
00:08:11,594 --> 00:08:15,113
Next: how Aboriginal and Torres
Strait Islander Peoples

189
00:08:15,274 --> 00:08:18,633
have used solubility
to remove toxins from food.

190
00:08:19,834 --> 00:08:21,273
Here are the key notes.

191
00:08:21,514 --> 00:08:23,433
Pause the video if you need more time

192
00:08:23,514 --> 00:08:24,313
to copy them.
